Let's start with thinking about what would happen if we kept $A$ constant and changed $B$.

The expression has two terms, and only one of them depends on $B$.

$\begin{align}
\boxed{A}&+\boxed{\dfrac2B}
\\
\nearrow\quad&\phantom{+}\quad\nwarrow
\\
\text{doesn't depend on B}\quad&\quad\text{depends on B}
\end{align}$

What happens to $\dfrac2B$ when we increase or decrease $B$'s value?

Only one of the terms depends on $A$:

$\begin{align}
\boxed{A}&+\boxed{\dfrac2B}
\\
\nearrow\quad&\phantom{+}\quad\nwarrow
\\
\text{depends on A}\quad&\quad\text{doesn't depend on A}
\end{align}$

The term that depends on $A$ is simply $A$, so increasing it will increase the term, which in turn will increase the entire expression.
